===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}

Oracle bone script: {{liushu|p}}: a jade artifact with carved patterns. Original form of {{och-l|彫|to carve}}. The character was later borrowed as a country name and {{och-l|*口}} was added as a distinguishing mark. A semi-conservative version in which the carvings are visible is {{och-l|𠕛}}. The top part of the character gradually became similar to {{och-l|*用}} in the late Western Zhou period.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref> See also {{och-l|*黃}}, which could have a reference to a jade artifact.

Folk etymology interprets the seal script form as {{och-l|*口|mouth}} + {{och-l|*用|to use}}, indicating the idea of using words skillfully to express oneself, thereby implying the meaning of being meticulous or detailed.<ref>Digital Shinjigen 2017</ref>

===Etymology 1===
{{zh-forms|alt=𠕛,𠱬,𠄗,𠕛,𠣘,淍,週}}

;to encircle, to circle, to surround, everywhere, curve (in the road)
:Unclear. Possibly connected with {{cog|bo|གཅུ་བ}} ~ {{m|bo|ལྕུ་བ|t=screw}}, {{m|bo|གཅུད་བ}} ~ {{m|bo|ལྕུབ་བ|t=turn, twist, plait, braid}}, or {{cog|th|ดิ้ว||club, stick; strips of rattan or bamboo bent in a circle to which ribs of a cage are fastened}} or {{cog|km|ជួត||to wind; to wrap around; to wear a turban}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}})

;to help
:Unclear. Maybe same word as {{zh-l|周|to surround}} as words for "help, aid" often derive from the notion "next to, or around, a person" (for analogies, {{och-l|佐|to assist < to be on one's left-side}} from {{och-l|左|left|id=1}} and {{och-l|佑|to assist < to be on one's right-side}} from {{och-l|右||right|id=1}}) ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}).

:Alternatively, note {{cog|km|ជួយ||to help, assist; to support; to rescue, save}} (ibid.). Even so, also note {{cog|th|ช่วย}}, which in turn is from {{w|Middle Chinese}} {{ltc-l|助}}); if {{cog|km|ជួយ}} and {{cog|th|ช่วย}} were related, then {{cog|km|ជួយ}} cannot be related to {{w|Old Chinese}} {{och-l|周}}.

;dynasty's name
:Attested in the Huayuanzhang East {{w|Oracle Bone}} Inscription HYZ 327.1 (transliterated and translated by Schwartz, 2019<ref>{{cite-book|author=Schwartz, A.|title=The Oracle Bone Inscriptions from Huayuanzhuang East: Translated with an Introduction and Commentary|year=2019|isbn=9781501505294|doi=https://doi.org/10.1515/9781501505294|location=Boston, Berlin|publisher=De Gruyter Mouton}}</ref>)
::{{zh-x|^周 入 亖{sì}|'''Zhou''' contributes four|CL-PC|ref=}}

:A {{lang|zh|周方白}} (''Zhōufāng bó'', “Elder of Zhou region”), likely {{w|King Wen of Zhou}}, was mentioned in Oracle Bone Inscriptions H11:82 and H11:84 (Lin, 1995)<ref>Lin, Sen-Shou. 1995. “Problems in the Studies of Zhou Oracle-Bone Scripts.” ''Retrospective Theses and Dissertations'', 1919-2007. T, University of British Columbia. doi:http://dx.doi.org/10.14288/1.0098998.</ref>

:Hargett (2021)<ref>{{cite-journal|author= Hargett, James|year= 2021|title= Anchors of Stability: Place-Names in Early China|journal=Sino-Platonic Papers|editor=Mair, Victor|issue=312|titleurl=http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp312_chinese_place_names.pdf}}</ref> states that {{zh-l|^商}} and {{zh-l|^周}} derived from names of "places that the rulers and people of those eras regarded as their homeland or cultic centers"; the {{zh-l|周方|tr=Zhōufāng|Zhou region}} was among "areas or regions along the frontier borders of Shang, traditionally regarded as enemy territory".

