===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}

In oracle bone inscriptions, {{liushu|ic|nocap=yes}}: {{zh-l|*吅|loudly}} + ''person with long hair'' (Similar to {{zh-l|*老}} without the cane) – "cry; weep". The pictogram of the person with long hair corrupted into {{zh-l|*犬}}. Evidence suggests {{zh-l|*吅}} could be an abbreviation of the sound component {{och-l|咢}}, making the character {{liushu|psc|adj=yse|nocap=yes}}.

{{w|Shuowen Jiezi}} interprets it as a {{Han compound|吅|獄|c1=s|c2=p|t1=to speak loudly|abb2=y|ls=psc|nocap=1}}.

===Etymology===
From {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*kuːk||to weep; to wail}}; cognate with {{cog|lus|kûk||to shriek}} (STEDT).

This seems to be an area word; compare {{cog|mkh-pro|*kuk|*kuk ~ kuuk ~ kuək ~ kək|to call}}, {{cog|map-pro|*-kuk||sound of a sob}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}).

